    "summary": "The Pasero Opioid-induced Sedation Scale (POSS) rates how sleepy a patient on opioids is, because too much sedation comes before respiratory depression.",
    "facts": ["S = asleep, easy to rouse; 1 = awake and alert; 2 = slightly drowsy, easily roused. These are acceptable.", "3 = often drowsy, drifts off mid-conversation: unacceptable. Hold or reduce the opioid (often by 25–50%) as ordered and monitor closely.", "4 = minimal or no response to voice or touch: stop the opioid, support breathing and consider naloxone.", "Check sedation with respiratory rate, depth and SpO₂. Many policies say hold the opioid and report if the respiratory rate is under 10–12 a minute."],
