    "summary": "Three things that are often mixed up. Tolerance and physical dependence are expected body changes with regular opioid use; opioid use disorder (OUD) is a disease of compulsive use.",
    "facts": ["Tolerance: a higher dose is needed for the same effect. It develops to sedation, nausea and breathing depression, but not to constipation or small pupils.", "Physical dependence: withdrawal if the drug is stopped suddenly or an antagonist is given (yawning, runny nose, sweating, goosebumps, dilated pupils, diarrhoea, cramps). Prevent it by tapering.", "Opioid use disorder: craving, loss of control and continued use despite harm. Treated with medicines for OUD (buprenorphine, methadone or naltrexone) plus support."],
