"notes": {
  "intro": "Pain is whatever the patient says it is. Nurses assess it (type, score, goal), choose the least invasive drug that will control it, check safety before giving it (allergies, the rights, breathing and sedation for opioids), then reassess: about 30 minutes after an IV dose and 60 minutes after an oral dose. The WHO ladder matches drug strength to pain: non-opioids for mild pain, weak opioids for moderate pain, strong opioids for severe pain, with adjuvants at any step. This chapter also covers muscle relaxants, gout drugs and local anaesthetics. Where the Open RN book is dated, these cards follow current guidance (CDC 2022 opioid guideline, FDA warnings) and say so.",
  "sections": [
    { "cat": "assess", "about": "Acute pain has a clear cause and fades with healing; chronic pain outlasts healing (over 6 months in the book, over 3 months in current guidance). Nociceptive pain comes from damaged tissue; neuropathic pain from damaged nerves. Score pain with a 0–10 scale or FACES, and for opioids also score sedation with POSS, because sleepiness comes before slowed breathing.",
      "points": ["0–10: 1–3 mild, 4–6 moderate, 7–10 severe. Self-report is the gold standard.", "POSS: S, 1 and 2 are fine; 3 = hold or reduce; 4 = stop the opioid, consider naloxone.", "Tolerance and physical dependence are expected with regular use; opioid use disorder is compulsive use despite harm."],
      "tip": "WHO ladder = 1-2-3: 'Non, Weak, Strong'. Mild climbs one rung, severe can jump straight to the top." },
    { "cat": "nonop", "about": "Non-opioids block prostaglandin production. Acetaminophen acts mainly in the brain: it relieves pain and fever but not inflammation, and its danger is the liver. NSAIDs (aspirin, ibuprofen, ketorolac, celecoxib) also fight inflammation; their dangers are the stomach, kidneys, bleeding and the heart.",
      "points": ["Acetaminophen: no more than 4 g a day from all sources; antidote acetylcysteine.", "Aspirin: platelets blocked for 7–10 days; no aspirin for children with viral illness (Reye's).", "NSAIDs: take with food; boxed warning for heart attack, stroke and GI bleeding; ketorolac for 5 days at most; celecoxib spares the stomach but not the heart."],
      "tip": "Acetaminophen hurts the Liver; NSAIDs hurt the 'SKB': Stomach, Kidneys, Blood (and heart)." },
    { "cat": "opioid", "about": "Opioids bind mu receptors in the brain and spinal cord. Weak opioids (codeine, tramadol) sit on step 2; strong opioids (morphine, hydromorphone, oxycodone, fentanyl) on step 3. All can cause respiratory depression, sedation, nausea, itching, urinary retention and constipation. Naloxone reverses them but wears off sooner. The CDC 2022 guideline prefers non-opioids where they work, and when opioids are needed advises immediate-release at the lowest effective dose for no longer than needed, particular caution with benzodiazepines and other CNS depressants, and offering naloxone to people at higher risk.",
      "points": ["Before every dose: pain score, respiratory rate, SpO₂ and sedation. Start a laxative with any regular opioid.", "Hydromorphone is about 5–7 times stronger per mg than morphine; fentanyl patches are only for opioid-tolerant patients.", "Codeine and tramadol rely on CYP2D6: not for children under 12."],
      "tip": "Opioid side effects spell 'CONSTIPATED': Constipation, Orthostatic low BP, Nausea, Sedation, Tolerance, Itching, Pupils pinpoint, Altered mind, Too-slow breathing, Emesis, Dependence." },
    { "cat": "adjuv", "about": "Adjuvant analgesics are drugs made for other conditions that also ease some kinds of pain, especially nerve pain. Gabapentin (an anticonvulsant) and amitriptyline (a tricyclic) calm the nerves; lidocaine numbs them by blocking sodium channels. The chapter's anaesthetics section also covers propofol (a white IV emulsion that puts patients to sleep within about 40 seconds but gives no pain relief) and midazolam with fentanyl for moderate (procedural) sedation.",
      "points": ["Gabapentin: drowsiness and dizziness; extra breathing risk with opioids; taper.", "Amitriptyline: low dose at bedtime; anticholinergic effects.", "Lidocaine patch 12 hours on, 12 off; EMLA about an hour before needles; protect numb skin."],
      "tip": "Adjuvants help when nerves are the problem: 'Gabby Ami numbs with Lido'." },
    { "cat": "relax", "about": "Baclofen, cyclobenzaprine and tizanidine act in the CNS. Baclofen and tizanidine treat spasticity from MS or spinal cord injury; cyclobenzaprine treats short-term spasm from a strain. Dantrolene acts inside the muscle and is the antidote for malignant hyperthermia. The three CNS-acting relaxants cause drowsiness and dizziness: avoid alcohol and other CNS depressants, and rise slowly.",
      "points": ["Baclofen: GABA-B in the spinal cord; never stop suddenly.", "Cyclobenzaprine: tricyclic-like, anticholinergic, 2–3 weeks; never with MAOIs.", "Tizanidine: alpha-2 agonist; low BP and liver tests. Dantrolene: blocks calcium release in muscle."],
      "tip": "Baclofen = Back (spine) of the body; Dantrolene = Direct on muscle; Tizanidine = Tension down and blood pressure down." },
    { "cat": "gout", "about": "Gout is inflammation caused by urate crystals, usually starting in the big toe. Colchicine (or an NSAID or a steroid) treats the flare. Allopurinol (and febuxostat) block xanthine oxidase to make less uric acid; probenecid helps the kidneys excrete more. Both long-term drugs need plenty of fluid to prevent kidney stones.",
      "points": ["Colchicine: anti-inflammatory, not a painkiller; diarrhoea = toxicity; no grapefruit.", "Allopurinol: daily for prevention, not for an active flare; stop at the first rash.", "Probenecid: drink 2–3 L a day; avoid aspirin; not for kidney stones."],
      "tip": "Colchicine Calms the flare; Allopurinol Avoids uric acid being made; Probenecid Pushes it out in the Pee." }
  ]
}
